;; Endeavour 159 Phase B rung 01 — the NVIDIA sync MMA emits an fp16 instruction.
;;
;; THE DEFECT THIS STATES.  Crisp cannot emit a 16-bit MMA on NVIDIA at all.  Both PTX paths
;; are hardcoded tf32: sync `%emit-nvvm-mma` pins llvm.nvvm.mma.m16n8k8.row.col.tf32
;; (src/mma.lisp:748), and the PTX branch of analyze-make-register-fragment (src/mma.lisp:389)
;; ecase's on OPERAND only, ignoring :elem entirely.  Its own docstring says so:
;;
;;     "The PTX branch is UNCHANGED: its fragment records are tf32/f32 by construction and
;;      endeavour 155 does not touch the NVIDIA path."
;;
;; So endeavour 155 built the typed-shape machinery (%mma-elem-bits, %mma-shape-for-elem, the
;; :elem keyword) and deliberately wired it to SPV only.  This rung is the NVIDIA twin of
;; 155/02, and it is the LOWEST rung that can be: the sync m16n8k16 instruction, one warp, one
;; output tile, nothing pipelined.
;;
;; THE TARGET INSTRUCTION, VERIFIED BY COMPILATION NOT BY DOCUMENTATION.  A standalone .ll probe
;; through clang --target=nvptx64 -march=sm_90 establishes the exact NVVM intrinsic:
;;
;;     llvm.nvvm.mma.m16n8k16.row.col.f32.f32   operands <2 x half>   ->
;;         mma.sync.aligned.m16n8k16.row.col.f32.f16.f16.f32
;;
;; NOTE THE OPERAND TYPE, because it is the trap on this rung: fp16 takes <2 x half> while the
;; bf16 sibling (llvm.nvvm.mma.m16n8k16.row.col.bf16) takes i32.  A single "16-bit" code path
;; that assumes one silently fails the verifier on the other.  Worse, two plausible-looking
;; intrinsic spellings (...f16.f32, ...bf16.f32) slip past the verifier as UNRESOLVED EXTERNAL
;; CALLS: they emit NO instruction while still leaving an `mma.m16n8k16...` string in the PTX.
;; The validator below therefore asserts the FULL instruction mnemonic, not a prefix.
;;
;; ARITY IS UNCHANGED, which is why this rung is smaller than it looks.  tf32 m16n8k8 and fp16
;; m16n8k16 both take 4 A registers + 2 B registers + 4 f32 accumulator.  So endeavour 144's
;; register accounting (:acc 4 :a 4 :b 2) is already correct at 16 bits, and the f32 accumulator
;; record is reused unchanged.  Only the A/B fragment records need 16-bit twins.
;;
;; WHY THE ASSERTION IS TWO-SIDED.  155/02 recorded the lesson directly: a module where MOST
;; operands were still f32 satisfied a "some operand is 16-bit somewhere" check, and that weak
;; form is what let the defect survive.  So this validator asserts the INVARIANT -- the f16
;; instruction is present AND no tf32 MMA remains -- rather than mere existence.
;;
;; --hardware-profile IS REQUIRED, and not because of the shape.  Recorded by 155/02 and it
;; binds every register-tile spec: "load-tile into a register-tile requires a hardware profile
;; (pass --hardware-profile): its GRF / L1 limits drive the register-pipeline safety analysis."
;; The h100 profile already declares (16 8 16) in :mma-shapes -- the shape has been registered
;; since endeavour 144 and nothing has ever emitted it.
;;
;; ===================================================================
;; STATUS: RED ON PURPOSE.  159 sorts after ci-stop (158-prefetch-warps), so this does not
;; break CI.  It goes green when the PTX branch honours :elem end to end.  Metal correctness is
;; NOT claimed here -- that waits for the single batched pod trip (MMA_CORRECT via TEST-HOIST).
;; ===================================================================
;;
;;
;; WHY THIS RUNG IS SKIPPED UNDER --differentiate, and it is a GAP CLAIM, not a shrug.
;; The forward is correct: the module carries exactly one
;; mma.sync.aligned.m16n8k16.row.col.f32.fp16.fp16.f32.  The AD-generated *_grad kernel
;; alongside it carries THREE mma.sync.aligned.m16n8k8.row.col.f32.tf32.tf32.f32 -- the
;; backward mints TF32 fragments for a 16-bit forward.
;;
;; That is a CORRECTNESS bug and not a precision one, which the emitted PTX settles:
;; there is not a single fp16->f32 conversion in the grad kernel (its only cvt
;; instructions are integer index arithmetic), and each tf32 mma is fed directly by
;; `ld.shared.b32` out of shared memory holding PACKED 16-BIT data.  A tf32 m16n8k8
;; fragment expects 32-bit elements, so the backward reads the wrong bytes and the
;; gradient is garbage rather than merely coarse.
;;
;; The fix is to thread the operand element type into the MMA VJP's fragment minting --
;; the sync-path analogue of what endeavour 160 did for wgmma -- and it needs an
;; on-metal numeric gradient check (VERIFY-AUTODIFF) to confirm, so it is its own piece
;; of work.  Filed as BUG 054.
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; TEST-WITH[--ir-target=ptx --hardware-profile=h100] : validate-ptx-fp16-sync-mma

(def-type a-mat (matrix half  :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))
(def-type b-mat (matrix half  :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :col-major))
(def-type c-mat (matrix float :address-space :global :align :compact :contiguous-term :row-major))

;; WHY THE STAGED (SLM) PATTERN AND NOT 155/02's REGISTER-RESIDENT ONE.  This rung was first
;; written as a direct twin of 155/02, with A and B as `make-register-tile ... :operand :a/:b`.
;; The compiler refuses that on NVIDIA, and says exactly why:
;;
;;     load-tile into a register-tile lowers to Subgroup2DBlockLoadINTEL, which is Intel/SPV-only
;;     -- it has no PTX/NVIDIA mapping in this register-pipeline model.
;;
;; Endeavour 142 Phase A's register-resident operand path is INTEL-ONLY.  On NVIDIA the operands
;; stage through SLM: scratch matrices for A and B, a register tile for C only -- the shape 150/20
;; and 135/03 already use.  So this rung mirrors 150/20's kernel with the element type and K-step
;; swapped, which is the narrowest possible change and keeps the fp16 instruction the ONLY thing
;; under test.
;;
;; K-STEP IS 16, NOT 8.  tf32 m16n8k8 walks K in steps of 8; fp16 m16n8k16 walks it in 16.  That
;; is the whole ladder step -- K doubles, M and N do not -- and it is why the fp16 ceiling is ~2x
;; tf32 rather than 4x (Phase A measured 1.05-2.16x on H100 NVL and the prediction held).

(def-kernel fp16_sync_mma (A B &out C)
  (declare #'(a-mat b-mat &out c-mat)
           (global-size :set-to 32)
           (local-size  :set-to 32))          ; one warp
  (let ((A-tile (make-scratch-matrix half (16 16)))   ; SLM: M x 16 (one fp16 K-step of A)
        (B-tile (make-scratch-matrix half (16 8)))    ; SLM: 16 x N (one fp16 K-step of B)
        (C-tile (make-register-tile float (16 8) 0.0))
        (K      (inner-dimension A B)))
    (matrix-multiply-tile-stride C C-tile K 16 (grid-y grid-x grid-k)
      (load-tile A A-tile (grid-y grid-k))
      (load-tile B B-tile (grid-k grid-x))
      (sync-workgroup)
      (mma-accumulate-via-tile (16 8 16) C-tile A-tile B-tile)
      (sync-workgroup)
      :epilogue
      (store-tile C-tile C (grid-y grid-x)))))
